# Supervised Resources

A Plugin can run one supervised process for as long as its Agent runs. In this tutorial you build a ticket-number allocator, and then see how it starts, how handlers reach it, and how it recovers. Run the cells in order.

## Install Jido

```elixir
Mix.install([
  {:jido, github: "mikehostetler/new_jido", branch: "main"}
])
```

## Write the runtime process

The process starts from a `Jido.Plugin.Init`. This value carries the owning server, the Agent id, the Plugin's options, and the committed value of its field. It is everything a replacement process needs to rebuild itself.

```elixir
defmodule ResourceGuide.Allocator do
  use GenServer

  alias Jido.Plugin.Init

  def start_link(%Init{} = init), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, init)
  def next(runtime), do: GenServer.call(runtime, :next)
  def committed(runtime), do: GenServer.call(runtime, :committed)

  @impl true
  def init(%Init{} = init) do
    state = %{init: init, prefix: init.options[:prefix], last: init.plugin_state}
    {:ok, state}
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:next, _from, state) do
    number = state.last + 1
    {:reply, {:ok, "#{state.prefix}-#{number}", number}, %{state | last: number}}
  end

  def handle_call(:committed, _from, state) do
    {:reply, Jido.AgentServer.Plugin.state(state.init), state}
  end
end
```

`Jido.AgentServer.Plugin.state/2` reads the current committed value. `init.plugin_state` is only the value at startup.

## Write the Plugin

`child_spec/1` tells the Activation how to start the process. `await_ready/2` runs after every Plugin's process has started. `validate_options/1` rejects bad options before anything starts. `admit/3` receives the running process as its first argument.

```elixir
defmodule ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers do
  use Jido.Plugin

  alias ResourceGuide.Allocator
  alias Jido.Agent.Plugin.Reduction
  alias Jido.Plugin.Context.Admission
  alias Jido.Plugin.Init

  @impl true
  def validate_options(opts) do
    if is_binary(opts[:prefix]) and opts[:prefix] != "",
      do: :ok,
      else: {:error, Jido.Error.validation_error("Ticket prefix is required", kind: :config)}
  end

  @impl true
  def child_spec(%Init{} = init) do
    %{id: __MODULE__, start: {Allocator, :start_link, [init]}}
  end

  @impl true
  def await_ready(runtime, _opts) do
    case Allocator.committed(runtime) do
      {:ok, _value} -> :ok
      error -> error
    end
  end

  @impl true
  def state_spec(_opts), do: {:last_number, Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)}

  @impl true
  def admit(runtime, %Admission{signal: %{type: "ticket.open"}}, _opts) do
    with {:ok, label, number} <- Allocator.next(runtime) do
      {:ok, %{label: label, number: number}}
    end
  end

  def admit(_runtime, %Admission{}, _opts), do: {:ok, nil}

  @impl true
  def reduce(%Reduction{} = reduction, _opts) do
    {:ok, max(reduction.plugin_state, reduction.candidate_state.number)}
  end
end

defmodule ResourceGuide.Ticket do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "ticket",
    plugins: [{ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers, %{prefix: "TCK"}}]

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{
             label: Jido.Joi.string() |> Jido.Joi.default(""),
             number: Jido.Joi.integer() |> Jido.Joi.default(0)
           })
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/support/ticket"

    route "ticket.open", as: :open do
      action _input, schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{}), context: context do
        %{label: label, number: number} = context.plugin_inputs[ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers].runtime
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | label: label, number: number}}
      end
    end
  end
end

ticket_plugin = ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers

Jido.Agent.define(%{
  name: "no_prefix",
  plugins: [%{id: ticket_plugin.id(), provider: ticket_plugin, vsn: ticket_plugin.vsn(), data: %{}}]
})
|> elem(1)
|> Map.get(:message)
```

The cell returns `"Ticket prefix is required"`.

## Start it

An AgentServer starts the process under its Activation. `Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` returns after every Plugin process has started and every `await_ready/2` has returned `:ok`, which must happen within `readiness_timeout`, 5,000 milliseconds by default.

```elixir
{:ok, _instance} = Jido.start(name: ResourceGuide.Jido)
{:ok, server} = Jido.start_agent(ResourceGuide.Jido, ResourceGuide.Ticket, id: "desk-1")
:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(server)

{:ok, %{state: first}, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, ResourceGuide.Ticket.open_signal!(%{}))
{:ok, %{state: second}, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(server, ResourceGuide.Ticket.open_signal!(%{}))

{first.label, second.label, second.last_number}
```

The cell returns `{"TCK-1", "TCK-2", 2}`.

A Plugin with `child_spec/1` needs an Activation. `Jido.start_agent/3`, `Jido.AgentServer.start/1`, and `Jido.AgentServer.child_spec/1` provide one. `Jido.AgentServer.start_link/1` returns an error for such an Agent.

A startup failure stops the Activation. That covers an invalid child specification, a failed start, an `await_ready/2` that returns anything other than `:ok`, and a readiness timeout. `Jido.start_agent/3` can return `{:ok, pid}` first, and `Jido.AgentServer.await_ready/2` then exits, so always call it before relying on a new server. The runtime root must use the default `:permanent` restart. Any other value is an invalid specification.

## See it recover

The Plugin's process and the AgentServer belong to one Activation, and the Activation restarts them as a group. When the process dies, a new server starts from the last commit, and the Plugin starts from a new `Jido.Plugin.Init`.

The child id `{:plugin, module}` identifies the Plugin's process under `Jido.AgentServer.activation/1`.

```elixir
activation = Jido.AgentServer.activation(server)

{{:plugin, ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers}, allocator, _kind, _modules} =
  activation
  |> Supervisor.which_children()
  |> List.keyfind({:plugin, ResourceGuide.TicketNumbers}, 0)

Process.exit(allocator, :kill)

replacement =
  Enum.find_value(1..200, fn _attempt ->
    Process.sleep(10)
    pid = Jido.whereis_agent(ResourceGuide.Jido, "desk-1")
    if pid not in [nil, server], do: pid
  end)

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(replacement)

{:ok, %{state: third}, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(replacement, ResourceGuide.Ticket.open_signal!(%{}))
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(ResourceGuide.Jido, replacement)

{replacement != server, third.label}
```

The cell returns `{true, "TCK-3"}`. The number continued because the new process read `last_number` from its `Jido.Plugin.Init`. A number that the old process handed out without a commit would be issued again, so keep anything you cannot lose in the owned field.

The Activation allows 3 restarts in 5 seconds, then stops. To keep a failing leaf from restarting the Agent, return a `Supervisor` as the root and supervise the leaf under it. The root stays up while the leaf restarts, and the Agent is not disturbed.

## Keep secrets out of the definition

Plugin options are static data stored in the Agent definition. Two features keep secrets and runtime terms out of it.

A `Jido.Plugin.Config` reference stores the name of an environment variable. `Jido.Plugin.Config.env/1` creates one without reading the environment, and `use Jido.Agent` imports it as `env/1` for DSL sections in the module body. The runtime calls `Jido.Plugin.Config.resolve/1` when it starts and keeps the resolved value to itself. The definition and any snapshot keep only the reference.

```elixir
System.put_env("RESOURCE_GUIDE_API_KEY", "private-key")

defmodule ResourceGuide.ConnectionRuntime do
  use GenServer

  def start_link(init), do: GenServer.start_link(__MODULE__, init)

  @impl true
  def init(init) do
    case Jido.Plugin.Config.resolve(init.options) do
      {:ok, options} -> {:ok, options}
      {:error, error} -> {:stop, error}
    end
  end

  @impl true
  def handle_call(:key_length, _from, options), do: {:reply, {:ok, byte_size(options[:api_key])}, options}
end

defmodule ResourceGuide.Connection do
  use Jido.Plugin, option_keys: [agent_server: [:api_key, :region]]

  @impl true
  def child_spec(init), do: %{id: __MODULE__, start: {ResourceGuide.ConnectionRuntime, :start_link, [init]}}

  @impl true
  def admit(runtime, _admission, opts) do
    with {:ok, length} <- GenServer.call(runtime, :key_length), do: {:ok, %{length: length, region: opts[:region]}}
  end
end

defmodule ResourceGuide.Gateway do
  use Jido.Agent,
    name: "gateway",
    plugins: [{ResourceGuide.Connection, %{api_key: Jido.Plugin.Config.env("RESOURCE_GUIDE_API_KEY"), region: "us"}}]

  state do
    schema Jido.Joi.object(%{seen: Jido.Joi.any() |> Jido.Joi.default(nil)})
  end

  routes do
    signal_source "/gateway"

    route "gateway.ping", as: :ping do
      action _input, schema: Jido.Joi.object(%{}), context: context do
        {:ok, %{context.agent_state | seen: context.plugin_inputs[ResourceGuide.Connection].runtime}}
      end
    end
  end
end

gateway_agent = ResourceGuide.Gateway.instantiate!(id: "gw-1")
{:ok, snapshot} = Jido.Persistence.snapshot(gateway_agent)
false = inspect(snapshot) =~ "private-key"

{:ok, gateway} =
  Jido.start_agent(ResourceGuide.Jido, ResourceGuide.Gateway,
    id: "gw-2",
    plugin_runtime_options: [{ResourceGuide.Connection, [region: "eu"]}]
  )

:ok = Jido.AgentServer.await_ready(gateway)
{:ok, %{state: %{seen: seen}}, []} = Jido.AgentServer.call(gateway, ResourceGuide.Gateway.ping_signal!(%{}))
:ok = Jido.stop_agent(ResourceGuide.Jido, gateway)
seen
```

The cell returns `%{length: 11, region: "eu"}`.

A missing variable makes `resolve/1` return a `Jido.Error.ValidationError` whose details have `code: :missing_env`. The runtime above then stops, and startup fails.

`plugin_runtime_options` is the second feature. It supplies options for one server start and merges them over the declared options, and `validate_options/1` then checks the result. These values never enter the definition, so they can hold terms such as pids. Each key must appear in the Plugin's `option_keys` for `:agent_server` and for no other owner. Any other key is an error, so a Plugin without `option_keys` accepts none.

